const engineeringSyllabi = {
  'SQL': r'''
Query relational data with validated joins, analytical windows and repeatable reporting controls. | Define and validate table grain. | Compose queries that preserve correct totals. | Diagnose plans and publish retry-safe aggregates. | Basic tables; examples use SQLite 3. | SQLite, SQL | Publish a monthly sales mart with customer rankings, source reconciliation and a retry-safe daily load.
# Tables and types | Establish row grain and explicit output columns. | Model orders and order lines and test both unique keys.
Keys and grain | Grain defines what a row represents. An order-line table needs an identifier per line, not just an order ID. A primary key protects that identity and rejects duplicate values. | PRIMARY KEY; Row grain; Constraint | CREATE TABLE orders(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, amount INTEGER);\nINSERT INTO orders VALUES(1,20),(2,45);\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders; | 2 | Commerce systems distinguish order headers from their items. | Insert ID 1 again; explain the failure and redesign for multiple lines per order.
Projection and aliases | SELECT defines output columns and expressions. AS labels the result without changing the source schema. Explicit column names protect consumers against accidental schema changes. | SELECT; AS; Output contract | SELECT 45 AS amount, 'USD' AS currency; | One row with amount 45 and currency USD. | Finance exports expose predictable column names. | Add a tax column and document its numeric unit.
? What is the grain of one row per product in an order? | Order line | Customer | Calendar month | One order can have multiple product lines.
? What prevents duplicate order identifiers? | A primary key | An alias | ORDER BY | A key enforces uniqueness at the stored grain.
# Predicates and NULL | Filter records and handle missingness explicitly. | Build a report including unknown amounts as a separate category.
Missing comparisons | A comparison to NULL produces unknown. Use IS NULL to find missing data. WHERE retains only true predicates, which explains why ordinary comparisons omit unknown values. | WHERE; IS NULL; Three-valued logic | WITH t(n) AS (VALUES(5),(NULL)) SELECT COUNT(*) FROM t WHERE n IS NULL; | 1 | Data quality checks count absent invoice amounts. | Compare n = NULL and n IS NULL on the same data.
Conditional groups | CASE evaluates conditions in order and emits the first match. Define ELSE so unexpected values remain visible rather than silently disappearing. | CASE; WHEN; ELSE | WITH t(n) AS (VALUES(25),(NULL)) SELECT CASE WHEN n IS NULL THEN 'unknown' WHEN n>=20 THEN 'high' ELSE 'low' END FROM t; | high and unknown | Operations reports classify response delays. | Add a medium bucket and test its boundaries.
? How do you select missing amounts? | amount IS NULL | amount = NULL | amount > NULL | IS NULL tests missingness directly.
? Why put the missing-value condition before numeric buckets? | It makes handling of unknown values explicit | It fills missing values with zero | It changes the source type | CASE defines a visible missing category without inventing values.
# Relationships and joins | Preserve unmatched rows and prevent fanout. | Join customers and shipments and reconcile row counts.
Unmatched keys | LEFT JOIN retains every left row. A right-side predicate in WHERE can remove unmatched rows, so decide whether it belongs in ON or a final exception filter. | LEFT JOIN; ON; Unmatched records | WITH c(id) AS (VALUES(1),(2)),o(cid) AS (VALUES(1)) SELECT c.id FROM c LEFT JOIN o ON c.id=o.cid WHERE o.cid IS NULL; | 2 | CRM teams find customers without orders. | Add two orders for ID 1 and compare row counts.
Semijoins | EXISTS tests for at least one matching row without repeating the parent. It is useful when child multiplicity should not change the output grain. | EXISTS; Cardinality; Fanout | WITH c(id) AS (VALUES(1),(2)),o(cid) AS (VALUES(1),(1)) SELECT c.id FROM c WHERE EXISTS(SELECT 1 FROM o WHERE o.cid=c.id); | One row with ID 1. | Revenue teams avoid multiplying header totals by item counts. | Compare EXISTS with an inner join for this fixture.
? Which join preserves customers without shipments? | LEFT JOIN from customers | INNER JOIN | CROSS JOIN | The left input retains unmatched rows.
? Which construction selects parents with matches without duplicating them? | EXISTS | CROSS JOIN | Sorting the join | EXISTS returns a Boolean condition per parent.
# Aggregates and rates | Group at the correct grain and reconcile denominators. | Create store totals, valid-value counts and missing rates.
Group filters | GROUP BY reduces records to group keys. HAVING filters aggregate results; WHERE filters the input before aggregation. | GROUP BY; HAVING; SUM | WITH t(s,n) AS (VALUES('A',5),('A',4),('B',3)) SELECT s,SUM(n) FROM t GROUP BY s HAVING SUM(n)>5; | A, 9 | Merchandising identifies stores above a volume threshold. | Exclude negative records before grouping.
Counts and division | COUNT(*) counts records and COUNT(column) counts known values. Promote integer division when calculating ratios and guard a zero denominator. | COUNT; NULLIF; Decimal division | WITH t(n) AS (VALUES(5),(NULL),(7)) SELECT COUNT(*),COUNT(n),1.0*COUNT(n)/NULLIF(COUNT(*),0) FROM t; | 3, 2 and about 0.667 | Quality teams measure field availability. | Return the missing rate and test an empty table.
? Which clause filters by an aggregate total? | HAVING | WHERE | AS | HAVING runs at group-result scope.
? COUNT(n) for 5, NULL and 7 is what? | 2 | 3 | 12 | Column counts omit NULL.
# Query composition | Build named intermediate results and combine sources. | Stage two monthly extracts and document duplicates.
Common table expressions | WITH gives an intermediate query a name within one statement. Give each stage one purpose and inspect its grain before adding further joins. | WITH; CTE; Intermediate grain | WITH lines(q,p) AS (VALUES(2,4),(1,9)),totals AS (SELECT q*p AS n FROM lines) SELECT SUM(n) FROM totals; | 17 | Analytics engineering makes transformations reviewable. | Add a separate tax stage with explicit output names.
Set operations | UNION removes equal result rows, while UNION ALL retains every occurrence. Both match columns by position, so select the same semantic fields in the same order. | UNION; UNION ALL; Deduplication | SELECT 'A' AS source UNION ALL SELECT 'A'; | Two rows containing A. | Ingestion combines partitions without hiding repeated events. | Compare UNION and explain why deduplicating by event ID differs.
? Which operator preserves duplicates? | UNION ALL | UNION | INTERSECT | UNION ALL appends every occurrence.
? What should be verified before joining a CTE of order totals? | One row per order key | Its label color | Descending display order only | Unique keys protect the intended join grain.
# Window analytics | Rank and accumulate without discarding detail. | Find the newest event per customer and daily running revenue.
Ranking | ROW_NUMBER assigns positions within a partition. Include a unique tie-breaker in ORDER BY so equal timestamps do not produce unstable survivors. | ROW_NUMBER; PARTITION BY; Tie-breaker | WITH t(g,n) AS (VALUES('A',10),('A',20),('B',5)) SELECT g,n,ROW_NUMBER() OVER(PARTITION BY g ORDER BY n DESC) FROM t; | A20 rank 1, A10 rank 2 and B5 rank 1. | CRM pipelines retain the latest customer status. | Add event IDs to break repeated timestamp ties.
Running frames | A ROWS frame accumulates a specified sequence of rows. Define its ordering explicitly; a default RANGE frame may handle tied order values differently. | SUM OVER; ROWS; Frame | WITH t(d,n) AS (VALUES(1,8),(2,3),(3,4)) SELECT d,SUM(n) OVER(ORDER BY d ROWS UNBOUNDED PRECEDING) FROM t; | 8, 11, 15 | Treasury dashboards display cumulative cash movement. | Partition the running total by account.
? Which feature computes ranks while preserving detail rows? | A window function | GROUP BY | DISTINCT | Window results attach to existing rows.
? What is the second cumulative total for 8, 3, 4? | 11 | 3 | 15 | The second frame contains 8 and 3.
# Plans and transactions | Inspect access strategies and preserve atomic writes. | Compare a plan with and without an index and roll back a failed change.
Index plans | EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN exposes scans and searches. Indexes trade storage and write overhead for potentially faster selective reads; the optimizer chooses based on the workload. | Index; Selectivity; Query plan | CREATE TABLE event(id INTEGER,status TEXT);\nCREATE INDEX event_status ON event(status);\nEXPLAIN QUERY PLAN SELECT id FROM event WHERE status='open'; | A search using event_status is expected. | Operations systems tune frequently filtered incident queries. | Remove the index and compare the plan on realistic data volumes.
Atomic changes | BEGIN groups related writes. COMMIT makes them durable under database guarantees; ROLLBACK discards uncommitted changes when validation fails. | BEGIN; COMMIT; ROLLBACK | CREATE TABLE stock(n INTEGER);\nINSERT INTO stock VALUES(8);\nBEGIN; UPDATE stock SET n=0; ROLLBACK;\nSELECT n FROM stock; | 8 | Fulfillment keeps stock and shipment updates consistent. | Roll back a two-table transfer when its control total fails.
? What does ROLLBACK cancel? | Uncommitted transaction changes | Every historical backup | All indexes automatically | It reverses changes in the current transaction.
? Why might a table scan be chosen despite an index? | Most rows match and indexed reads may cost more | Indexes force all queries to fail | All scans are sorted | Index usefulness depends on selectivity and cost.
# Reliable reporting | Make retries safe and publish reconciled totals. | Deliver a daily sales load with a unique key and exception queries.
Idempotent upserts | Use a uniqueness constraint and replacement semantics for retryable aggregates. Appending the same total twice is not idempotent. | ON CONFLICT; Unique key; Retry | CREATE TABLE daily(day TEXT PRIMARY KEY,n INTEGER);\nINSERT INTO daily VALUES('2026-01-01',4);\nINSERT INTO daily VALUES('2026-01-01',4) ON CONFLICT(day) DO UPDATE SET n=excluded.n;\nSELECT SUM(n) FROM daily; | 4 | Warehouse loads recover from repeated job submissions. | Execute the upsert three times and check row count and total.
Reconciliation exceptions | Compare source and served metrics at the same grain and scope. An exception query should return no rows when all controls agree. | Reconciliation; Grain; Delta | WITH e(n) AS (VALUES(40)),a(n) AS (VALUES(39)) SELECT e.n-a.n FROM e,a WHERE e.n<>a.n; | One exception with delta 1. | Finance teams approve a report after independent checks. | Add region checks to catch offsetting errors hidden in a grand total.
? What makes a daily aggregate retry safe? | Replace the value for the same unique date | Append it each retry | Remove its date | Repeated identical replacements leave state unchanged.
? Expected total 40 versus published 39 should produce what? | A discrepancy of 1 | A success with no exception | A total of 79 | The difference requires investigation.
