Select and test efficient structures and algorithms for practical software problems. | Analyze time and space growth. | Implement collection, tree and graph algorithms. | Verify correctness and benchmark realistic workloads. | Python functions and loops. | Python 3, collections, heapq | Build a route and scheduling toolkit with unit tests, complexity notes and a measured performance report.
# Complexity and contracts | Define accepted input and estimate dominant work. | Compare nested scans with a keyed solution across increasing inputs.
Growth rates | Two full nested loops perform n squared iterations. Focus on the dominant term while retaining constant factors in measured performance. | Big O; Dominant term; Space | n=10\nprint(sum(1 for i in range(n) for j in range(n))) | 100 | Code reviews identify quadratic ingestion logic. | Double n and compare operation count and elapsed time.
Boundary contracts | Document empty input, invalid types and duplicate behavior before optimizing. Assertions need an expected answer, not merely absence of a crash. | Preconditions; Empty input; Assertion | def first_or_none(a): return a[0] if a else None\nassert first_or_none([]) is None | Assertion passes. | Batch processing handles valid empty extracts. | Test a singleton, tuple and invalid input according to your contract.
? Doubling n changes n squared work by what factor? | Four | Two | One | Squaring 2n multiplies n squared by four.
? What determines the correct empty-input result? | The documented function contract | Chart color | The last random run | Tests must reflect deliberate boundary behavior.
# Arrays and hashing | Replace repeated scans with appropriate indexing. | Count event frequencies and find a target pair in sorted amounts.
Frequency maps | A hash map normally offers constant-time average lookup. Updating a count in one pass avoids a full scan per distinct value, at the cost of additional memory. | Hash map; Frequency; Average complexity | from collections import Counter\nprint(dict(Counter(['A','B','A']))) | {'A': 2, 'B': 1} | Event pipelines count regional activity. | Analyze memory when all values differ.
Two pointers | On sorted values, increasing the left pointer increases the sum and decreasing the right pointer decreases it. This invariant lets a full target-pair algorithm discard candidates safely. | Sorted invariant; Opposite pointers | a=[1,3,5,8]; lo,hi=0,3\nwhile lo<hi and a[lo]+a[hi]<11: lo+=1\nprint(a[lo]+a[hi]==11) | True | Reconciliation pairs positive sorted amounts. | Implement both movement directions and return None if pointers meet.
? What is a hash map's typical average lookup cost? | O(1) | O(n squared) | Always O(log n) | Hash lookup generally avoids a full key scan.
? Which condition enables the target-sum two-pointer invariant? | Sorted input | Exactly four records | All records unique | Sorting makes pointer changes monotone.
# Stacks and queues | Match processing order to problem structure. | Validate brackets and simulate a FIFO task queue.
Stack order | A stack is last-in-first-out. For brackets, track unmatched openings and reject a closing symbol when the stack is empty or mismatched. | LIFO; Push; Pop | s=[]\nfor ch in '(())':\n    if ch=='(': s.append(ch)\n    else: s.pop()\nprint(not s) | True for this valid fixture. | Parsers interpret nested expressions. | Handle invalid input beginning with a closing bracket without underflow.
Queue order | A deque supports efficient removal from the front and addition at the back. FIFO preserves arrival order among equivalent priorities. | FIFO; deque; Retry | from collections import deque\nq=deque(['a','b']);q.append('c')\nprint(q.popleft(),list(q)) | a ['b', 'c'] | Job processors schedule waiting tasks fairly. | Append one failed job for retry and add a maximum attempt count.
? Which value leaves a stack first? | The last pushed | The first pushed | The smallest | LIFO reverses the arrival sequence.
? Which value leaves a FIFO queue first? | Earliest waiting item | Latest arrival | Largest value automatically | FIFO retains arrival order.
# Searching and sorting | Use ordering to reduce work and control ties. | Implement a lower-bound search and verify duplicate handling.
Lower bounds | Binary search halves a sorted candidate interval. A lower bound returns the first location whose value is at least the target, including the insertion point if absent. | bisect_left; Sorted data; Bound | from bisect import bisect_left\nprint(bisect_left([1,3,3,9],3)) | 1 | Tiered pricing locates a threshold efficiently. | Test targets below the minimum and above the maximum.
Stable sorting | Stability preserves input order among equal keys. Use a secondary key when reproducibility must hold even across different original input orders. | Stable sort; Key; Tie-breaker | print(sorted([('b',1),('a',1),('c',0)],key=lambda r:r[1])) | [('c', 0), ('b', 1), ('a', 1)] | Ticket sorting preserves arrival order for equal priority. | Sort by priority and then ID and compare the tie behavior.
? What is the first index of a value at least 3 in [1,3,3,9]? | 1 | 2 | 3 | A lower bound chooses the first matching position.
? What does sort stability preserve? | Relative order of equal keys | Every original index | Unsorted values | Equal-key entries keep their original relative sequence.
# Trees and heaps | Traverse hierarchy and maintain priorities. | Build a top-K selector and compare its memory with sorting all rows.
Inorder traversal | In a binary search tree, left keys precede the root and right keys follow it. Inorder emits sorted keys only when that ordering invariant is maintained. | BST; Left-root-right; Recursion | def walk(t): return [] if t is None else walk(t[1])+[t[0]]+walk(t[2])\nprint(walk((2,(1,None,None),(3,None,None)))) | [1, 2, 3] | Indexes enumerate records by increasing key. | Add a duplicate-key policy and test it.
Priority heaps | A min-heap exposes the smallest item without globally sorting its backing array. Insert and pop are logarithmic in heap size. | Min-heap; heapq; Priority | import heapq\na=[5,1,3];heapq.heapify(a)\nprint(heapq.heappop(a),a[0]) | 1 3 | Schedulers choose the next deadline. | Maintain the largest two values using a size-two min-heap.
? Which traversal yields sorted keys in a valid BST? | Inorder | Preorder | Arbitrary DFS | Left, root, right matches the BST ordering rule.
? What does a min-heap containing 5,1,3 pop? | 1 | 5 | 3 | The root is the minimum.
# Graph algorithms | Model reachability, costs and prerequisites. | Detect a dependency cycle and explain its effect on build order.
Breadth-first distances | BFS expands by edge count and finds shortest paths on unweighted graphs. Mark visited nodes when enqueuing to avoid duplicate frontier entries. | BFS; Queue; Distance | from collections import deque\nq=deque(['A']);d={'A':0};g={'A':['B'],'B':[]}\nwhile q:\n    u=q.popleft()\n    for v in g[u]:\n        if v not in d:d[v]=d[u]+1;q.append(v)\nprint(d) | {'A': 0, 'B': 1} | Service graphs expose dependency hops. | Add a disconnected component and report unreachable nodes.
Topological order | A DAG admits an ordering where prerequisites appear before dependants. A cycle makes that impossible because every member waits directly or indirectly on itself. | DAG; Indegree; Cycle | from graphlib import TopologicalSorter\nprint(tuple(TopologicalSorter({'build':{'lint'},'lint':set()}).static_order())) | ('lint', 'build') | CI systems order prerequisite jobs. | Introduce a cycle and handle the reported failure.
? What finds the fewest edges on an unweighted graph? | BFS | Unconstrained DFS | Sorting labels | BFS visits states in increasing hop count.
? Why is a dependency cycle unschedulable under strict prerequisites? | Every cycle member depends on itself indirectly | Keys must be integers | Cycles lack edges | There is no starting task that satisfies every dependency.
# Dynamic programming | Reuse subproblem results and define state transitions. | Solve a step-counting problem recursively and iteratively.
Memoization | Cache results using all state variables that affect the answer. Hidden mutable inputs can invalidate cached results and should be part of the state or avoided. | Cache; Recurrence; State | from functools import lru_cache\n@lru_cache(None)\ndef f(n):return n if n<2 else f(n-1)+f(n-2)\nprint(f(7)) | 13 | Allocation tools avoid recomputing overlapping subproblems. | Count unique states and compare with uncached recursion.
Tabulation | Compute predecessor states before their consumers. Choose base cases that reflect the exact meaning of an empty or initial state. | Bottom-up; Base case; Transition | ways=[1,1]\nfor i in range(2,6):ways.append(ways[-1]+ways[-2])\nprint(ways[5]) | 8 ways using steps of one or two. | Planning counts feasible transitions across discrete states. | Rework for step sizes one and three.
? What identifies a reusable memoized subproblem? | Its complete input state | The latest clock tick | Its output color | A cache key must identify all relevant input state.
? Why fill predecessor entries first? | Transitions read their computed values | It removes all memory use | It ensures random order | Dependencies must exist before they can be combined.
# Verification and trade-offs | Benchmark realistic workloads and test invariants. | Submit a correct implementation with resource measurements and edge-case tests.
Amortized membership | Building a set costs time and space but can pay off across many repeated membership queries. Preserve a separate list when original order matters. | Preprocessing; Amortization; Memory | valid=set(['a','b','c'])\nprint([x in valid for x in ['a','z']]) | [True, False] | Reference validation checks many foreign keys. | Compare build-plus-query costs at one and 100,000 queries.
Preservation properties | A sorting implementation must produce ordered values and preserve the input multiset. Checking order alone would accept an empty list for every input. | Invariant; Multiset; Oracle | from collections import Counter\na=[3,1,1];b=sorted(a)\nassert b==sorted(b) and Counter(a)==Counter(b) | Both assertions pass. | Custom ranking code is tested on duplicate-heavy batches. | Generate 100 seeded cases including empty arrays.
? When does building a reference set usually pay off? | Many membership checks use it | Input must stay in place with no memory | A single tiny query always | Query savings amortize construction costs.
? Which property catches dropped duplicates in sorting? | Equal input and output multisets | Sorted output alone | Output minimum alone | Multisets retain each value's multiplicity.
''',
